// Firmware presets pushed into the embedded editor.
//
// The code does not live here. Each board is a PlatformIO project under
// firmware/ -- that is the tree that gets flashed to hardware -- and the browser
// gets firmware/build/<name>.ino, a single-file bundle of exactly those sources
// generated by `node tools/bundle.mjs`. What you read in the editor is what
// runs on the board, and the bundle is compiled and flown in the host test
// before it is ever served here.
//
// The recommended board is the ESP32 DevKit V1: it is the networked part in this
// build (Xtensa LX6 dual-core, WiFi+BT) and the one proven to compile and run
// under the container's emulation. Boards report their own state over serial,
// which is what the ranch reads back -- the scene mirrors the board, not the
// other way round.

export const BOARD = 'ESP32 DevKit V1';
// The inspection board is not the same module as the other four: the OV2640 needs the
// S3's DVP pin count, and its platformio env is esp32-s3-devkitc-1.
export const BOARD_S3 = 'ESP32-S3-DevKitC-1 + OV2640';
// What is actually bolted to the show airframe. `board` above is the string the
// simulator's own board list is matched against, so it stays what that list says; this
// is the line the drawer prints, and naming a camera on an aircraft that carries an RTK
// receiver instead would be a label the BOM contradicts.
export const SHOW_KIT = 'ESP32-S3-DevKitC-1 + NEO-M9N RTK + 2×WS2812B';

